course: books
lesson: 59
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IX, part 4: Wayfarers All (the voyage and lunch by the road)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Sea Rat goes on with his voyage: down the coast of Italy to Palermo and happy weeks with friends in Sicily, then on to Sardinia and Corsica; he never sticks too long to one ship.
explain: When the Water Rat asks whether it isn't hot down in the ship's hold, the Sea Rat winks: «The captain's cabin's good enough for me» — he rides along as a guest, not as a hard-working sailor.
explain: At Alassio he watched wine-casks tied in a long line pulled ashore «like a mile of porpoises»; he rested in the olive-woods above the blue sea, and at Marseilles he feasted with old shipmates and still dreams of its shellfish.
explain: The polite Water Rat invites him to lunch; the Sea Rat asks to eat outside, so the Rat packs long French bread, a garlicky sausage, strong cheese and a flask from the sunny South, and blushes with pride when the old sailor praises his choice.
explain: Over lunch the Sea Rat tells the rest: the ports of Portugal and France, the harbours of Cornwall and Devon, and the last stormy quay where the first hints of spring sent him inland to try a quiet farm — which the Water Rat doesn't want to hear about at all.
fact: «Southwards we sailed again at last ... coasting down the Italian shore, till finally we made Palermo» (made = reached); Palermo is in Sicily, «one of my happy hunting-grounds», where he stayed «many jolly weeks» with friends.
fact: He says: «I never stick too long to one ship; one gets narrow-minded and prejudiced» (prejudiced = having fixed opinions before knowing the facts).
fact: Next he took «a ship that was trading to Sardinia and Corsica», glad «to feel the fresh breeze and the sea-spray in my face once more».
fact: The Water Rat asks if it is «very hot and stuffy, down in the — hold» (the hold is the inside of a ship where the cargo goes). The Sea Rat, with «the suspicion of a wink»: «The captain's cabin's good enough for me.»
fact: «It's a hard life, by all accounts,» says the Water Rat. «For the crew it is,» says the seafarer, «again with the ghost of a wink»: he doesn't work as crew; he rides in comfort, as ship's rats do.
fact: From Corsica a ship carried wine to the mainland; at Alassio (a little town on the Italian coast) they «hove» (threw) the wine-casks overboard, «tied one to the other by a long line», and the crew rowed ashore singing, pulling «the long bobbing procession of casks, like a mile of porpoises».
fact: On the sands, horses dragged the casks «up the steep street of the little town with a fine rush and clatter and scramble».
fact: Then he took «to the great olive-woods for a spell and a rest», «stretched high on the hillside with the blue Mediterranean far below me».
fact: At Marseilles (a big port in the south of France) he met old shipmates; «sometimes I dream of the shell-fish of Marseilles, and wake up crying!»
fact: The «polite Water Rat» invites him to lunch; the Sea Rat asks him to bring it outside: «I am none too fond of going under hatches» (indoors, below deck), and indoors «it is a hundred to one that I shall presently fall asleep».
fact: The Rat packs «a yard of long French bread, a sausage out of which the garlic sang, some cheese which lay down and cried, and a long-necked straw-covered flask wherein lay bottled sunshine shed and garnered on far Southern slopes»: jokes for a very garlicky sausage, a ripe runny cheese, and wine (a grown-ups' drink) from sunny southern vineyards.
fact: He chooses these «remembering the stranger's origin and preferences», and «blushed for pleasure at the old seaman's commendations» (praise).
fact: The voyage goes on «from port to port of Spain», to «Lisbon, Oporto, and Bordeaux»: Lisbon and Oporto are really in Portugal, and Bordeaux is in France.
fact: Then «the pleasant harbours of Cornwall and Devon» (in the south-west of England), «and so up the Channel to that final quayside», where, «storm-driven and weather-beaten», he caught «the first magical hints and heraldings of another Spring» and «sped on a long tramp inland» to try a quiet farm.
fact: The Water Rat follows «league by league» (a league is about three miles), and leaves him «planted at his dull inland farm, about which he desired to hear nothing».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «sweet as» (New Zealand) = great, fine; «howay» (North East England) = come on; «boss» (Liverpool) = great; «do the needful» (Indian English) = do what's needed; «can or not» (Singapore) = is it possible?
exercise: Which port did they reach after coasting down the Italian shore? | Palermo | Venice | Lisbon | Constantinople
exercise: On which island did the Sea Rat spend many jolly weeks with friends? | Sicily | Corsica | Cyprus | Crete
exercise: Why does he never stick too long to one ship? | One gets narrow-minded and prejudiced | The captains always throw him off in the end | He gets seasick if he stays on board too long | The food on any ship gets worse every week
exercise: What is the «hold» of a ship? | The inside, where the cargo goes | The handle that steers it | The rope that ties it up | The very top of the tallest mast
exercise: Where does the Sea Rat say he travels? | In the captain's cabin | Down in the hot hold | Up the mast in the crow's nest | In the ship's lifeboat, under a sail
exercise: «For the crew it is.» What does his wink tell us? | He isn't crew; he travels in comfort | He is the captain of the whole ship | He works harder than all the crew together | He has never been on a ship in his life
exercise: Where did the ship from Corsica take its cargo? | To Alassio on the mainland | Back to Constantinople | To the London River | Far up a winding river to Paris
exercise: What was the cargo? | Wine in casks | Shellfish in barrels | Bales of fine silk | Olives in jars
exercise: How did they get the casks ashore? | Threw them overboard tied in a line and rowed them in | Lifted them out with a great crane onto the quay | Carried them one by one up a long plank | Let the tide wash them up on the sand during the night, one by one
exercise: What did the line of bobbing casks look like? | A mile of porpoises | A string of pearls | A flock of ducks | A long floating bridge of logs
exercise: How were the casks taken up the steep street? | Horses dragged them | The crew rolled them | A steam engine pulled them | The townsfolk carried them
exercise: Where did the Sea Rat rest after Alassio? | In the great olive-woods above the sea | In a ruined castle on a lonely hill | In a gondola on the Grand Canal | In the captain's cabin of the wine ship
exercise: What does he dream of from Marseilles? | Its shellfish | Its fine churches | Its racing horses | Its snowy winters
exercise: Why does the Sea Rat want lunch outside, not in the Rat's hole? | He dislikes going «under hatches» and would fall asleep | He is afraid of meeting the Mole and the Badger | The Rat's hole is too small for a sea-going rat | He wants to keep an eye on the road in case the police come by looking for tramps
exercise: What does «under hatches» mean here? | Indoors, below deck | Under a hen | Under arrest | Under the sea, with the fish
exercise: «A sausage out of which the garlic sang». What does it mean? | It smelt strongly of garlic | It played a little tune | It was very small | It was burnt black over the fire
exercise: «Some cheese which lay down and cried». What does it mean? | It was so ripe it was runny and strong | It was very sad and lonely | It was hard as a stone | It fell off the table onto the floor
exercise: What was in the long-necked straw-covered flask? | Wine from sunny southern slopes | Cold milk from the farm | Fresh river water | Hot sweet tea with milk and sugar
exercise: Why did the Rat choose these foods? | He remembered where the stranger came from | They were all he had in the cupboard | The Mole had bought them by mistake | The swallows told him that sailors like such food
exercise: How did the Rat feel when the old seaman praised his choice? | He blushed for pleasure | He felt cross and embarrassed | He didn't hear the praise | He laughed at the seaman
exercise: Which harbours of England does the Sea Rat mention? | Cornwall and Devon | Kent and Sussex | London and Dover | Yorkshire and Hull
exercise: What made him leave the last quayside and go inland? | The first hints of another Spring | A storm that wrecked his ship | An order from the captain | A long letter from his family in Constantinople
exercise: How far is a league? | About three miles | About a yard or so | About a hundred miles | About a single step
exercise: How does the Water Rat feel about the inland farm part? | He doesn't want to hear about it at all | It is his favourite part of the story | He asks to go and visit the farm | He thinks the farm sounds very exciting indeed
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I, what was in the Rat's first luncheon-basket? | «cold chicken ... coldtonguecoldhamcoldbeef...» and more | A yard of French bread and garlic sausage | Bubble-and-squeak and buttered toast | Sardines, captain's biscuits and a German sausage in silver paper
exercise: Looking back: in chapter I, who begged to unpack the luncheon-basket and gasped «O my! O my!»? | The Mole | The Otter | Toad, at the Hall | Badger, from the wood
exercise: Looking back: in chapter V, how did the Rat get supper for the field-mice at Mole End? | Sent the field-mouse with the lantern shopping | Cooked the Mole's last German sausage for everyone | Rowed home to fetch his own luncheon-basket | Asked Badger to send food through his tunnels
exercise: ★★★ The book says «from port to port of Spain», then names Lisbon, Oporto and Bordeaux. Which is true about those three? | None is in Spain: two are in Portugal, one in France | All three are in Spain, on its north coast | Lisbon is in Spain; the others are in Italy | They are three small islands out in the Mediterranean Sea
exercise: ★★★ Count the islands the Sea Rat visits in this part: Sicily, Sardinia, Corsica. How many? | Three | Only two | About four | Only one
test: «The Sea Rat works his paws off as a sailor, sweet as.» (sweet as = fine) Put it right. | He winks: he rides in the captain's cabin; the crew work | Yes, he scrubs the decks and hauls ropes all day | He is the captain and gives all the orders | He stays on land and never sails at all, being afraid of water
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | The Sea Rat asks for lunch to be brought outside | The Sea Rat goes into the Rat's hole and sleeps | The Water Rat refuses to give him any lunch | The Mole joins them for lunch by the road and tells about Mole End
test: «Howay, the Rat just grabbed whatever was in the cupboard.» (howay = come on) Put it right. | He chose food to suit the stranger's southern tastes | He bought the food from the Stores | The Mole packed it, not the Rat | He brought nothing but a dry crust of bread and some water
test: Why are «the garlic sang» and «the cheese lay down and cried» funny? | Smells are described as if food could sing and cry | Garlic really can sing if it is fresh | The cheese was crying because it was sad | The Rat made the food sing for fun, by magic
test: Why does the Sea Rat stop mid-sentence: «Well, well, we won't linger over that now» and later «my pangs have been extreme»? | Talking about food has made him very hungry | He has a pain in his leg from walking | He is sad about his family troubles | He suddenly wants to lie down and sleep
test: Kev from Liverpool: «boss story, the Sea Rat stayed at sea and never touched land.» (boss = great) Put it right. | He loves the jolly times on shore and stays weeks with friends | Yes, he never once set foot on land | He hates the sea and stays on land | He only ever sails on quiet rivers and canals
test: What would the Sea Rat probably do if the Rat invited him indoors to his cosy parlour? | Fall asleep within minutes, he says | Steal the Rat's furniture | Stay for a year | Refuse to eat anything at all indoors
test: «Rat, do the needful and get lunch, yaar.» (do the needful = do what's needed; yaar = mate) What did the Rat do? | Fetched a basket and packed a meal | Sent the Mole for food | Cooked a big hot dinner indoors | Ordered a hot dinner from the Red Lion inn
test: «The farm part is the best bit, can or not?» (can or not = is that so?) What did the Water Rat think? | He didn't want to hear about the dull inland farm | He thought the farm was the most exciting part | He asked lots of questions about the cows | He decided there and then to become a farmer too
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Rat said other animals called him «a mean beast» who cut it «very fine». Was that fair? | No: his lunches are always generous, then and now | Yes: he always packs too little food | Yes: he never invites anyone | No: he never takes any lunch anywhere at all
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, Badger said he hates Society. How is the Sea Rat different? | He loves company, friends in every port, and feasts | He also hates company and lives alone | He lives underground like Badger | He never talks to anyone he meets on his travels
test: Looking back: which other traveller in this book loved speed and machines, not ships? | Toad, with his motor-cars | The Mole, with his boat | Badger, with his lantern | Otter, with his long nights of fishing
test: Looking back: in chapter IX, part 2, the swallows said spring calls them back to England. What called the Sea Rat inland? | The «first magical hints ... of another Spring» | The smell of the field-mice's flats | A letter from Toad Hall | The bells of Venice ringing over the water
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, Toad saw fields and cows «flying past» from the engine. How does the Water Rat travel today? | Only in his mind, «league by league» with the story | On a fast ship to Marseilles | On the same engine as Toad | In his little boat, all the way down to the sea
test: ★★★ «like a mile of porpoises». Why is that a good picture? | Round casks bobbing in a long line look like porpoises leaping | Porpoises really carry wine casks for sailors | The casks were painted grey with fins | A mile is the usual length of a big grey porpoise
test: ★★★ Careful: the Sea Rat says he left the sea when spring came, and tried the farm for «six months». What season should it be now — and is that what the chapter shows? | Late summer, turning to autumn — yes, the birds are leaving | Midwinter — yes, it is snowing | Early spring — yes, the cuckoo sings | Midsummer — yes, it is the shortest night of the year
exam: Identify the method by which the casks of wine were conveyed ashore at Alassio. | They were cast overboard, linked by a line, and drawn ashore by boats | They were lowered upon a crane to a quay constructed for the purpose | They were rolled down a gangway by the crew one at a time | They were transferred to a smaller vessel and conveyed up a river to the warehouses of the town
exam: State the reason adduced by the seafarer for his reluctance to partake of luncheon indoors. | His aversion to going under hatches and the likelihood of his falling asleep | His fear of encountering the officers of the law within the house | The insufficiency of the Water Rat's accommodation for a guest | His intention of departing before the meal was concluded, he having an appointment in the south
exam: Ascertain which of the following was NOT included in the luncheon provided by the Water Rat. | Shellfish from Marseilles | French bread | A sausage of garlic | A long-necked straw-covered flask
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of the seafarer's last voyage? | Having landed upon a quayside in the Channel, he proceeded inland upon the first hints of spring | Having been shipwrecked off Cornwall, he swam ashore and was taken in by farmers | Having arrived at Venice, he resolved to settle permanently upon the Grand Canal | Having reached Marseilles, he was conveyed to England by train and thence by coach to the farm
